messages:
sarah: Quick helm question — we have a chart that needs to populate env vars from a mix of an ExternalSecret (managed by external-secrets) and a fallback values.yaml string. Terraform started rotating secret names (suffixes), so sometimes the ExternalSecret-created Secret isn't present at template/render time. What is the best pattern to: prefer the k8s Secret when present, otherwise use a plaintext value from .Values? Also we want to include an additional configmap via envFrom. Any short examples? :thinking:

ajay: You can use the `lookup` builtin to detect if the Secret exists and then render either a valueFrom block or a plain value. Keep it small and robust. Example pattern:```yaml
{{- $sname := default (printf "%s-rotated" .Values.appName) .Values.externalSecretName }}
{{- $secret := lookup "v1" "Secret" .Release.Namespace $sname }}
env:
{{- if $secret }}
- name: DB_PASS
  valueFrom:
    secretKeyRef:
      name: {{ $sname }}
      key: db_password
{{- else }}
- name: DB_PASS
  value: {{ .Values.db_password | quote }}
{{- end }}
```
This avoids `required` failing the whole install when the secret is missing.

leo: If your secret name itself is templated (e.g. uses release or env), wrap it with `tpl` before lookup:```{{- $sname := tpl .Values.externalSecretName . -}}``` Also prefer `toYaml` + `nindent` for longer env lists so indentation stays correct. If you need to merge multiple sources you can just put multiple entries under `envFrom` (Secret then ConfigMap). K8s will prefer specific env entries over envFrom if names collide.

sarah: Good point about `tpl`. Does `lookup` ever cause helm to fail the render stage?

ajay: No, `lookup` returns nil if not found. Just check with `if $secret` before using it. Avoid `required` on the lookup result. Also watch for RBAC: the user running `helm template` or the Tillerless rendering must have permission to list Secrets if you're doing server-side lookup. For pure client-side rendering without cluster access, `lookup` will return nil and you'll fall back to the .Values default (which is fine).

leo: Short extra: if you want a non-failing lint but still want to catch regressions, add a helm test or a CI check that runs `kubectl get secret $NAME -n $NS` after Terraform apply, or have Terraform export the expected name into values so helm and TF agree.

sarah: Thanks! One more: we also want to include a configmap with generic envs. Is this pattern valid?
```yaml
envFrom:
- secretRef:
    name: {{ $sname }}
- configMapRef:
    name: {{ .Values.extraConfigMap | default "app-config" }}
```
Will that silently skip the missing secret entry?

ajay: If `envFrom` references a secret that doesn't exist at apply time, the k8s API will error on apply. So either keep the conditional on the whole `envFrom` entry (only add secretRef if lookup returns a Secret), or render a small env list where you fallback to plain `value` entries like the DB_PASS example. Example conditional `envFrom` rendering:```yaml
envFrom:
{{- if $secret }}
- secretRef:
    name: {{ $sname }}
{{- end }}
- configMapRef:
    name: {{ .Values.extraConfigMap | default "app-config" }}
```
That way the manifest won't reference a missing secret.

deploy-bot: helm lint: passed for chart/my-service (helm v3.10.0) :rocket: (no cluster lookup performed in CI)

sarah: Awesome, that's exactly what we needed. I'll update the chart to use `tpl` + `lookup` and conditionally add `envFrom` for the secret. Thanks all! :raised_hands:
